{{Japanese name|Oizumi}}

'''Oizumi Yu''' (大泉 勇, ''Ōizumi Yū''; born 12 April 1958) is a Sanese politician who served as the leader of the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP) from 2006 to 2021. He represented the [[Okami 5th District]] in the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] for 25 years until his defeat in the [[Okaiken general election, 2021|2021 general election]].

A former sociology professor and labor activist, Oizumi is credited with patiently rebuilding the OSP's credibility following its "Winter Period" of irrelevance. Under his leadership, the party grew from a marginal force into a significant parliamentary bloc, culminating in the 2016 election where it won 46 seats and entered government for the first time in decades.

He served as Minister of Labour in the coalition government of [[Sho Aoyagi]] (2016–2021). However, his decision to align the moderate OSP with the radical [[Sanese Communist Party]] (SCP) proved politically fatal. The coalition's unpopularity led to the OSP's near-total collapse in the 2021 election, where the party was reduced to two seats and Oizumi lost his own constituency. He is often cited by political analysts as a "tragic architect"—a leader who spent decades building a party only to oversee its destruction.

==Early life and academic career==
Oizumi was born in 1958 in a working-class district of [[Okami]]. His father was a railway worker and a local union representative, which exposed Oizumi to labor politics from a young age. He attended [[Okami University]], graduating with a degree in Sociology in 1981.

Before entering politics, Oizumi worked as an academic. He lectured at [[Toyozawa University]], specializing in the changing dynamics of the Sanese urban workforce. He was a frequent commentator in leftist journals, arguing that the socialist movement needed to move away from the rigid statism of the [[Genzui Kusaka]] era and embrace a more flexible, democratic socialism.

==Political career==
===Rise to leadership===
Oizumi was first elected to the Diet in the [[1996 Okaiken general election]], winning the [[Okami 5th District]] by a narrow margin. He quickly established himself as the intellectual leader of the OSP's moderate wing. He was critical of the party leadership's nostalgic attachment to the past, arguing that the OSP had to apologize for the "Kusaka years" if it ever wanted to govern again.

Following the resignation of party leader [[Tatsuo Akamatsu]] in 2006, Oizumi was elected as his successor. He inherited a party that was stagnant and polling in the single digits.

===The Rebuilding Project (2006–2016)===
Oizumi's leadership style was unflashy and pragmatic. He focused on grassroots organization and recruiting credible candidates from civil society. He slowly rebranded the OSP as the party of "responsible compassion," distancing it from the revolutionary rhetoric of the Communists.

His strategy yielded gradual results. The party held its ground in 2009 and gained seats in 2013. In the [[Okaiken general election, 2016|2016 general election]], the OSP achieved its best result in fifty years, winning 46 seats and nearly 10% of the popular vote. This surge made the OSP indispensable to the forming of a non-LDP government.

===Coalition Government (2016–2021)===
In July 2016, Oizumi made the controversial decision to join a coalition government led by the [[Sanese Communist Party]] (SCP) and its Prime Minister, [[Sho Aoyagi]]. Oizumi justified the alliance as the only way to end the LDP's dominance and enact social reforms. He was appointed '''Minister of Labour''', a portfolio he used to pass the "Fair Work Act" of 2018, which increased the minimum wage and strengthened protections for contract workers.

However, the coalition was fraught with tension. As the SCP government became embroiled in inflation scandals and broke its pacifist promises regarding defense spending, Oizumi faced pressure to withdraw. He refused, believing that stability was paramount. This decision alienated the OSP's moderate base, who felt Oizumi had become a "human shield" for the Communists' incompetence.

===2021 Collapse and Resignation===
The [[Okaiken general election, 2021|2021 general election]] was a catastrophe for Oizumi and his party. Punished by voters for their association with the unpopular Aoyagi administration, the OSP's support evaporated. The party lost 44 of its 46 seats.

In a final humiliation, Oizumi lost his own seat in Okami 5th District to a political newcomer from the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|PPO]]. On election night, a visibly shaken Oizumi announced his immediate resignation, apologizing to the remaining party members for "leading our house into the storm." He was succeeded by interim leader [[Koga Mitsuo]].

==Later life==
Since 2021, Oizumi has retired from public life, returning to writing and academia. He has notably refrained from commenting on the 2025 resurgence of the OSP under [[Rento Nakayama]], though private reports suggest he views Nakayama's populism with skepticism.

